infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title SSE vs WebSockets for token streams
  desc Token flow is one-directional — SSE's plain HTTP wins; WebSockets only when the client must push
  compares
    - label SSE — one-directional over plain HTTP
      children
        - label One-directional fits token flow
          desc Server → client only — exactly what a token stream is
        - label No upgrade handshake — plain HTTP
          desc Works through standard proxies and HTTP/2 multiplexing
        - label Trivially resumable
          desc EventSource auto-reconnects; Last-Event-ID resumes
        - label A fetch() away
          desc Read response.body with a parser, as every AI SDK does
    - label WebSockets — bidirectional, stateful
      children
        - label Bidirectional — client pushes too
          desc The one case that justifies the complexity
        - label Needs an upgrade handshake
          desc Not plain HTTP — proxies must support WebSockets
        - label Stateful long-lived connection
          desc More to operate; overkill for one-way token flow
        - label Only for frequent client→server messages
          desc When the client must push often over one connection
theme
  palette #8b5cf6 #f59e0b
